<section image-layout="blank" id="qPTo9Ra8PL" background-color="heading"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="j19AMP_Atx" vertical-align="center" colspan="35"><p role="eyebrow">History detective case · Southern Brazil · 1835–1845</p><h1 font-size="44pt">What can three objects tell us?</h1><p font-size="lg"><b>A tax paper. A folded map. A letter from home.</b></p><p>Each holds a clue about the Ragamuffin War, known in Portuguese as the <i>Revolução Farroupilha</i>.</p><spacer size="18pt"></spacer><labels><label variant="solid">Our mystery</label></labels><p font-size="lg">Why did it last from 1835 to 1845—and how did it affect different lives?</p></grid-item><grid-item id="B2xnv6g21v" padding="0" colspan="25"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/Yaffpv4CNHlZ4_f4Cqm8R.png" saved-media-id="41xc7hqhvpiodrl" source="image.ai-generated" background="true" image-style="southern-brazil-paper-museum" alt="Layered cut-paper museum-table illustration of three carefully arranged objects from nineteenth-century southern Brazil: a blank tax paper with wax seal, a folded schematic map with no lettering, and a tied letter from home, colored-pencil shading on warm cream paper, postcard red question marks as small paper cutouts, river blue and sun yellow accents, clear daylight, no people, no weapons, no legible text" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1248,&quot;height&quot;:1664}"></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="M1mbzey5q6"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="KNd-D_3P6d"><h2>First clue: where did it happen?</h2><p font-size="lg"><b>South America → Brazil → Rio Grande do Sul</b></p><freeform-block w="840" h="285"><shape preset="roundRect" w="240" h="275" fill="surface" stroke="palette-5" stroke-width="1"></shape><shape preset="teardrop" x="72" y="112" w="96" h="122" rotation="8" fill="palette-2" stroke-width="0"></shape><shape preset="ellipse" x="116" y="194" w="24" h="24" fill="accent" stroke-width="0"></shape><box type="content" x="20" y="18" w="200"><p role="eyebrow">1 · Continent</p><h3>South America</h3><p>Brazil is one country on this continent.</p></box><shape preset="rightArrow" x="250" y="124" w="38" h="26" fill="palette-3" stroke-width="0"></shape><shape preset="roundRect" x="300" w="240" h="275" fill="surface" stroke="palette-5" stroke-width="1"></shape><shape preset="teardrop" x="374" y="108" w="90" h="126" rotation="14" fill="palette-3" stroke-width="0"></shape><shape preset="ellipse" x="412" y="211" w="25" h="25" fill="accent" stroke-width="0"></shape><box type="content" x="320" y="18" w="200"><p role="eyebrow">2 · Country</p><h3>Brazil</h3><p>Look toward the far south of the country.</p></box><box type="content" x="377" y="239" w="116" h="25" vertical-align="center" sizing="fixed" background-color="accent" style="padding: 4pt 8pt; border-radius: 999pt;"><p font-size="sm" align="center">Southern Brazil</p></box><shape preset="rightArrow" x="550" y="124" w="38" h="26" fill="palette-3" stroke-width="0"></shape><shape preset="roundRect" x="600" w="240" h="275" fill="surface" stroke="palette-5" stroke-width="1"></shape><shape preset="roundRect" x="650" y="125" w="140" h="92" rotation="-3" fill="palette-2" stroke-width="0"></shape><shape preset="ellipse" x="703" y="154" w="34" h="34" fill="accent" stroke="palette-3" stroke-width="3"></shape><box type="content" x="620" y="18" w="200"><p role="eyebrow">3 · State</p><h3>Rio Grande do Sul</h3><p>The conflict was centered here.</p></box><box type="content" x="675" y="223" w="NaN"><p font-size="sm" align="center"><b>Story center</b></p></box></freeform-block><p font-size="sm"><b>Map note:</b> This classroom map is schematic, not an exact picture of historical borders. · Source: <a href="https://www.rs.gov.br/carta-de-servicos/servicos?servico=2268">Rio Grande do Sul government</a></p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="3qEGBRvkCe" vertical-align="center"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="OsBjJOLiBY"><h2>Why were people arguing?</h2><p font-size="lg"><b>There was not one simple cause.</b></p><grid marker="icon" marker-size="large" marker-style="solid" marker-shape="circle" marker-position="above" marker-align="center" marker-straddle="true" spacing="small" padding-top="38pt"><grid-item id="wRLGkSjpK5" cell-style="subtle" colspan="15"><grid-item-label><icon src="https://iconscdn.pictographic.ai/image/PSp1FDr6zxTYMizxh2Og.svg?stroke=45" source="image.pictographic.icon" alt="tax paper and coins" meta="{&quot;description&quot;:&quot;A sheet of paper folded in half, symbolizing writing, documentation, or office work.&quot;}"></icon></grid-item-label><h4>Trade and taxes</h4><p>People disputed economic rules, including taxes and trade.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="53M3K6Ds0O" cell-style="subtle" colspan="15"><grid-item-label><icon src="https://iconscdn.pictographic.ai/image/NUimptX9XOP3v0swscOa.svg?stroke=45" source="image.pictographic.icon" alt="regional map decision" meta="{&quot;description&quot;:&quot;A solid black square, embodying the concept of decision, suggests a straightforward and definitive choice, much like a checkbox being marked to indicate a clear selection or a firm stance being taken, implying simplicity and directness in the decision-making process.&quot;}"></icon></grid-item-label><h4>Regional power</h4><p>Leaders in Rio Grande do Sul wanted more say in decisions.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="9AS1N81jnj" cell-style="subtle" colspan="15"><grid-item-label><icon src="https://iconscdn.pictographic.ai/image/DuaKgIUCoO86djYHhCCy.svg?stroke=45" source="image.pictographic.icon" alt="separate government building" meta="{&quot;description&quot;:&quot;A black shape isolated on a white background, signifying the concept of separation through its distinct and disconnected presence.&quot;}"></icon></grid-item-label><h4>Political future</h4><p>Some rebels wanted a separate republic: a country with its own government.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="6Cr2V9OkzX" cell-style="subtle" colspan="15"><grid-item-label><icon src="https://iconscdn.pictographic.ai/image/aVohuPeDtwTB6MLCoLYH.svg?stroke=45" source="image.pictographic.icon" alt="connected country regions" meta="{&quot;description&quot;:&quot;A black shape of a country map outline in a simple form, set against a clean white background, symbolizing a nation or territory.&quot;}"></icon></grid-item-label><h4>Imperial government</h4><p>It wanted Brazil to remain together.</p></grid-item></grid><p><b>Different people made different choices for different reasons.</b> No whole side was simply good or bad.</p><p font-size="sm">Source: <a href="https://cultura.rs.gov.br/upload/arquivos/carga20210405/27100547-anais-ahrs-v-16-1.pdf">State Historical Archive of Rio Grande do Sul</a></p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="c53f8YksRC"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="P4D3ip60K5"><h2>A ten-year timeline</h2><labels><label variant="solid">1835 → 1845 · Ten years</label></labels><smart-layout variant="minimalTimelineWithBoxes" orientation="horizontal" cell-size="15"><smart-layout-item><h4>1835 · Conflict begins</h4><p>Political conflict begins in Rio Grande do Sul.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>1835–1845 · A long middle</h4><p>Disagreement continues while families and workers live through years of uncertainty.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>1845 · Conflict ends</h4><p>The ten-year conflict comes to an end.</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout><grid spacing="none" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="_BnO1QGdYr" background-color="palette-3" padding="14pt 18pt"><h4>Time check</h4><p>Ten years is longer than most children spend in primary school.</p></grid-item></grid></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="zWyPZoJcSu" vertical-align="center"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="aFWW1SrX26"><p role="eyebrow">Illustrated viewpoints—not real biographies</p><h2>The same years did not feel the same to everyone</h2><p font-size="lg"><b>People did not have equal rights or choices.</b></p><grid spacing="medium" image-aspect="square" image-shape="circle"><grid-item id="q6LQfu7ubs" colspan="15"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/gGD2C5OqPDa_aGW67kzph.png" saved-media-id="zfxsdimdmwxh2fq" source="image.ai-generated" image-style="southern-brazil-paper-museum" alt="Layered cut-paper and colored-pencil portrait of an illustrative free market seller named Rosa in 1830s–1840s southern Brazil, adult woman with medium-brown skin beside a simple produce basket and market cloth, historically grounded but non-specific clothing, restrained thoughtful expression, warm cream paper background, no text, no weapons" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1440,&quot;height&quot;:1440}"><h4>Rosa</h4><p font-size="sm"><b>Free market seller</b></p><p>She watches for changes to trade.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="0L8QreX9N2" colspan="15"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/lgxSyDvqSpL5nmoDRcVvx.png" saved-media-id="j9wxj1llgjnq80i" source="image.ai-generated" image-style="southern-brazil-paper-museum" alt="Layered cut-paper and colored-pencil portrait of an illustrative rural worker named Tomás in 1830s–1840s southern Brazil, adult man with tan skin beside a travel bag and food sack, historically grounded but non-specific work clothing, calm serious expression, warm cream paper background, no text, no weapons" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1440,&quot;height&quot;:1440}"><h4>Tomás</h4><p font-size="sm"><b>Rural worker</b></p><p>He thinks about work, food, and travel.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="fvvVFiQVdc" colspan="15"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/1g5c3NTv_hlHKIVrdYs4h.png" saved-media-id="faklibuhgmv1rgw" source="image.ai-generated" image-style="southern-brazil-paper-museum" alt="Layered cut-paper and colored-pencil portrait of an illustrative enslaved worker named Luzia in 1830s–1840s southern Brazil, adult Black woman standing with dignity beside folded cloth and a carrying basket, historically grounded but non-specific clothing, restrained direct expression, warm cream paper background, no chains, no violence, no text, no weapons" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1440,&quot;height&quot;:1440}"><h4>Luzia</h4><p font-size="sm"><b>Enslaved worker</b></p><p>She has far fewer rights and choices. The revolt does not end slavery.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="Ln_Vcd2hoJ" colspan="15"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/Eso7Aks0Yke-EPaifM4xy.png" saved-media-id="ev5nhqxkrdhzxps" source="image.ai-generated" image-style="southern-brazil-paper-museum" alt="Layered cut-paper and colored-pencil portrait of an illustrative child named Davi in 1830s–1840s southern Brazil, brown-skinned child listening while nearby adult hands hold an opened letter with no readable writing, historically grounded but non-specific clothing, curious restrained expression, warm cream paper background, no text, no weapons" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1440,&quot;height&quot;:1440}"><h4>Davi</h4><p font-size="sm"><b>Child</b></p><p>He hears news shaped by the adults around him.</p></grid-item></grid></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="maVkVYE7pw" vertical-align="center"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="4d23GEQAGt"><p role="eyebrow">Illustrated object reconstructions</p><h2>Everyday objects carry big questions</h2><grid spacing="small" cell-size="10" image-aspect="landscape" image-shape="rounded"><grid-item id="wW773i3Qcl" colspan="15"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/XQTLd20C7RL_p4c-gMTTi.png" saved-media-id="p3cnn68vgkswyyq" source="image.ai-generated" image-style="southern-brazil-paper-museum" alt="Layered cut-paper museum illustration of a nineteenth-century tax paper on warm cream paper beside a few plain coins and a red seal, colored-pencil shading, postcard red and sun yellow accents, no people, no legible text, no logos" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1920,&quot;height&quot;:1088}"><h4>Tax paper</h4><p>Who pays—and who decides the rules?</p></grid-item><grid-item id="_Og7SG9d30" colspan="15"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/3PnuHsD5EjGkp4xI6QlOm.png" saved-media-id="2rh0htpgbi8hp0l" source="image.ai-generated" image-style="southern-brazil-paper-museum" alt="Layered cut-paper museum illustration of a folded schematic map on warm cream paper, river-blue route lines and one postcard-red region marker, visibly folded paper edges, no exact borders, no labels, no legible text" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1920,&quot;height&quot;:1088}"><h4>Map</h4><p>Who should govern the region?</p></grid-item><grid-item id="v39gtA3XYn" colspan="15"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/qvqut_qXqFkECEzKki72H.png" saved-media-id="rfp0asqdqgesx26" source="image.ai-generated" image-style="southern-brazil-paper-museum" alt="Layered cut-paper museum illustration of a tied letter from home opened beside a travel bag on warm cream paper, soft colored-pencil shading, river-blue ribbon and postcard-red wax seal, no readable writing, no people" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1920,&quot;height&quot;:1088}"><h4>Letter</h4><p>How do people learn what is happening?</p></grid-item><grid-item id="IXQBsr8SKv" colspan="15"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/_oUAvg-7ZvT2Jxg1zW_n5.png" saved-media-id="p43967s4w8dgccc" source="image.ai-generated" image-style="southern-brazil-paper-museum" alt="Layered cut-paper museum illustration of a practical food basket holding bread, dried beans and simple produce on warm cream paper, colored-pencil shading with sun-yellow and river-blue cloth accents, ordinary household scale, no text, no people" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1920,&quot;height&quot;:1088}"><h4>Food basket</h4><p>How do families keep daily life going?</p></grid-item></grid><p><b>Objects help us study political ideas and ordinary experiences together.</b></p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="xMdwlYaDbP"><grid spacing="medium"><grid-item id="8H1rDq98mq" background-overlay="tint" background-overlay-intensity="0.18" vertical-align="end" padding="24pt" colspan="22"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/FkgXYT8UJUI9N3ZoID4Q9.png" saved-media-id="wsi7wj2v1atjszo" source="image.ai-generated" background="true" image-style="southern-brazil-paper-museum" alt="Layered cut-paper museum-table illustration of an opened folded map reconnected to a tax paper, a tied letter and a food basket by one winding postcard-red paper thread, warm cream ground, river-blue geography marks and sun-yellow discovery tabs, clear daylight, no people, no flags, no weapons, no readable text" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1248,&quot;height&quot;:1664}"><labels><label variant="solid">Illustrated reconstruction</label></labels><h3>The objects reconnect</h3></grid-item><grid-item id="ZpCDqdc9Ob" colspan="38"><h2>What changed—and what did not?</h2><grid direction="vertical" spacing="small" vertical-sizing="fill"><grid-item id="2i56RtlhqF" cell-style="subtle"><labels><label variant="solid">Changed</label></labels><h3>The conflict stopped</h3><p>The ten-year conflict ended in 1845.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="VSx83LYBe6" cell-style="subtle"><labels><label variant="outline">Did not change</label></labels><ul><li>Rio Grande do Sul did not remain separate from Brazil.</li><li>Abolition was not an outcome of the revolt; slavery continued.</li></ul></grid-item></grid><grid spacing="none" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="sgVxkyKnSJ" background-color="accent" padding="14pt 18pt"><h4>Big idea</h4><p>Ending a conflict does not automatically make life fair for everyone.</p></grid-item></grid></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="DePWSpd22N" vertical-align="center"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="gQZ7gOjImT"><h2>History detective quiz</h2><p>Use the map, objects, timeline, and viewpoints to solve each question.</p><grid marker="number" marker-size="large" marker-style="solid" marker-shape="circle" marker-position="above" marker-align="start" spacing="medium" padding-top="34pt"><grid-item id="LBC_Z0oFrn" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><h4>Find the place</h4><p>Which Brazilian state is at the center of this story?</p></grid-item><grid-item id="e2ZRfMfON5" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><h4>Find the causes</h4><p>Name two kinds of disagreement behind the conflict.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="dnLxQ_9zgh" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><h4>Compare viewpoints</h4><p>Why might the same event feel different to a free seller and an enslaved worker?</p></grid-item></grid><grid spacing="none" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="Xg-f-AKEBD" background-color="surface" padding="12pt 16pt"><p><b>Check:</b> Rio Grande do Sul · trade and taxes, regional power, or republican separation · people did not have equal rights or choices.</p></grid-item></grid><grid spacing="none" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="NaetB0pnn8" background-color="accent" padding="16pt 18pt"><p role="eyebrow">Final detective question</p><h3>Whose voice might still be missing?</h3></grid-item></grid></grid-item></grid></section>